A||N||6||jeffozz||Subwoofer installation help??||||5||
Z||000000||jeffozz||07-08-2000||09:23 AM||||Here's the scoop...<BR>I have installed a later model Pontiac radio using an adapter harness and have lost correct function of the stock subwoofer (just makes a repeating noise).  The new radio is Pontiac model UP3.  If I unplug the new harness and plug the old radio back in, everything is back to normal...  Obviously, there's something I'm missing in the wiring, just don't know what it is??  Any help would be appreciated!<BR>Thanks;o)<BR>Jeff||||reg||5
Z||000001||BN Boomer||07-08-2000||10:59 AM||||Here's a link to an earlier discussion about this and how tgowens got his subwoofer to work with a later model radio. <A HREF="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/Forum1/HTML/002743.html" TARGET=_blank>Grand Am radio in a Fiero</A>. Might be a tip or two in there for you, too. Good luck. I just installed an AM/FM/CD player out of a 97 Sunfire in my 85 GT. I really like it. I don't have a subwoofer yet but I'm keeping my eye out for a good deal on one. <P><P>------------------<BR>Greg, aka Boomer<BR>Red 85 GT||||reg||1
Z||000002||tgowens||07-08-2000||12:57 PM||||Jeff, if you don't want to look up the thread.......do this, the stock radio power antenna leads won't power the sub - it sounds like a beating heart when you plug it in, right? -- you need to splice the two pink wires from the sub amp into the yellow wire on the wiring harness (that's right, BOTH pinks to one yellow) -- that's all there is to it.........the yellow on the harness actually mates to a white wire from the radio if you are using the adapter.......it's a power wire and will allow the unit to function in either the 'run' or 'accessory' position of the ignition.......good luck, hope this helps.......<P>------------------<BR>  <IMG SRC="http://www.tgowens.com/my_fiero_sig.gif">   <BR>'87 Fiero GT -  <A HREF="http://www.tgowens.com/fiero/" TARGET=_blank>www.tgowens.com/fiero</A>||||reg||1
Z||000003||tgowens||07-08-2000||01:15 PM||||oh by the way Jeff, something I forgot to tell you and Boomer, when I installed my radio, I put small spacers behind the mounting tabs (the new radio fits farther into the recess than the original stock radio did) to make the radio flush with the trim bezel when it is reinstalled......these can be found at the larger chain building supply stores (mine came from a Lowe's) -- I think I used 1/4" spacers......don't really remember but I will look at the actual size and post it for anybody interested......<P>Boomer, thanks for the mention and again, thanks for the Newell link, I'm sure Bobby appreciates it!<P>------------------<BR>  <IMG SRC="http://www.tgowens.com/my_fiero_sig.gif">   <BR>'87 Fiero GT -  <A HREF="http://www.tgowens.com/fiero/" TARGET=_blank>www.tgowens.com/fiero</A>||||reg||1
Z||000004||BN Boomer||07-08-2000||02:08 PM||||You welcome about the mention and Newell Electronics definitely deserves a few mentions from me. They were very helpful to me over just a $15 sale.<P>About the spacers, I used some thick nylon washers I came across at the hardware store. I'd have to double check to be sure, but I think they are 3/8" thick. They worked great and the radio is flush with the surface bezel. Looks good.||||reg||1
Z||000005||jeffozz||07-09-2000||12:34 AM||||Thanks for the help guys!  I was hoping it would be pretty simple...  I will let you know what happens!<BR>Again, thanks!<BR>Jeff;o)||||reg||7
